pseudo-Boolean proof version 2.0
* Regression test: the same asymmetric order as
* incorrect/version2/pre_order_asymmetric_left_right_vars, with the offending
* `left` moved one line past `end`.
*
* That earlier fix put the symmetry re-check where the `vars` block closes.
* But the rules that declare the lists were guarded only on the enclosing
* subcontext, and `end vars` does not close that subcontext -- it clears an
* `inside_vars` flag which `left`, `right`, `aux` and the `fresh_*` rules
* never read, even though `RuleOnlyAllowedInOrderVars`, the error they raise,
* says in so many words that they belong to "the variable definition of an
* order". Each rule overwrites its list outright, so a declaration written
* after `end vars` replaced the validated list with an unvalidated one and
* neither check saw it. `load_order bad x1 x2` was then sized against the
* poisoned `left` of length 2 while `right` still had length 1.
*
* Not a wrong verdict, and no attempt to build one from it succeeded: the
* truncated relation is still a genuine preorder, and `get_reflexivity_-
* substitution` truncates the same way, so a `def` that reaches the unpaired
* positions makes reflexivity unprovable. What is measured here is the bypass
* itself -- `load_order bad x1 x2` is accepted against a `right` of length 1,
* which is exactly the state both existing checks exist to prevent -- and the
* hazard it leaves live downstream, where `ActiveOrder::new` and both
* substitution getters zip the two sides with `izip!` and each stops at a
* different length. It is refused because a guard that can be stepped around
* by moving one line is not a guard.
f 1
pre_order bad
    vars
        left u1
        right v1
        aux
    end
    left u2 u3
    def
        1 ~u2 1 v1 >= 1 ;
    end
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1
        end
        proof
            proofgoal #1
                u >= 1 ;
            end -1
        end
    end
end
load_order bad x1 x2
output NONE
conclusion NONE
end pseudo-Boolean proof
